				"name": "your auntifa liza 🇵🇷  🦛 🦦",
				"url": "https://mastodon.social/@blogdiva",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/110/649/552/828/625/044/original/97dd263dd469a515.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-07-05T16:02:53.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> had to re-read your ¿? and, yup, read it backwards. sorry about that. my dyslexia swaps whole words. misunderstood it as sans-serif the default and changing it to serif.  </p><p>been changing fonts for so long, didn't remember serif is the default. back in the day, screen dottiness made it hard for me to read most serif body. </p><p>there's another reason for my hating serifs for body: 16px is too small. ligatures blend at that size. i prefer serifs for headings, sidebars, even emphasis.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "112734728579505988",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://social.coop/@jsit/112734728104148451",
			"author": {
				"name": "Jay 🆘",
				"url": "https://social.coop/@jsit",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/314/708/588/220/354/original/c83a44dc38e1a5d9.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-07-05T16:03:56.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> Every historical website and HTML document that didn’t specify a font would look different from the way it did when it was authored. I don’t like that idea.</p><p>Plus, as in vogue as sans-serifs are, serifs are still better for reading long text (for most people).</p><p>Is there a good reason to do this? Is it just because sans-serifs are cool? Might as well make browsers default to white text on black since everybody’s also obsessed with dark mode these days.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "112734735569362369",
			"parentId": "112734728579505988",
			"url": "https://social.coop/@jsit/112734734145333459",
			"author": {
				"name": "Jay 🆘",
				"url": "https://social.coop/@jsit",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/314/708/588/220/354/original/c83a44dc38e1a5d9.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-07-05T16:05:28.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> If browsers chose defaults based on what’s trendy, they would have changed their default font size to 9px in the mid 2000s.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "112734783229884428",
			"parentId": "112734717044161081",
			"url": "https://mastodon.online/@colepeters/112734783182869026",
			"author": {
				"name": "Cole Peters",
				"url": "https://mastodon.online/@colepeters",
				"avatarUrl": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/avatars/original/missing.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-07-05T16:17:56.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> I suppose if a layout was strictly dependent on the specific fallback's metrics? But even then, that layout would only be 'not broken' on platforms that made that specific fallback available 😅 (I realize this is all hypothetical and I don't have a strong opinion on it changing, just wanted to understand your point better)</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "112734802509182584",
			"parentId": "112734735569362369",
